# Never rebuild aws-lc (locked FIPS module)

`aws-lc-fips-sys` is **not** an ordinary Rust dependency. It compiles a
large C / CMake / Go-assembly cryptographic module — the **CMVP-validated
AWS-LC FIPS module (cert #4759)** that
[`enterprise-compliance.mdc`](enterprise-compliance.mdc) §4 pins as the
Track-A boundary. The workspace consumes it as a **locked, prebuilt
version from Amazon**: the module version never changes, because the
whole point of pinning a validated module is that its bytes are fixed.

Nothing we do to our own Rust source can change what that module
contains. Therefore **rebuilding it produces a bit-identical result and
buys nothing** — it only costs multiple minutes of wall clock, every
time, on every developer and lab host.

## The rule

> **Never cause `aws-lc-fips-sys` to rebuild. It is a locked prebuilt
> FIPS module; its output cannot change. If a debug build of it is ever
> genuinely unavoidable, build it exactly **once** and then never
> again — preserve the resulting
> `target/<profile>/build/aws-lc-fips-sys-*/out/` tree forever.**

## Why this keeps happening (the actual mechanism)

Cargo keys every build-script *execution* directory on a metadata hash:

```
target/<profile>/build/aws-lc-fips-sys-<HASH>/out/build/artifacts/
```

If the `<HASH>` changes, Cargo does **not** reuse the previous
directory — it runs the build script again from scratch, paying the full
C/CMake compile, and leaves the old directory behind as dead disk. The
hash is **not** derived from the crate's source (which is pinned); it is
derived from the *build environment*. The inputs that flip it:

| Invalidator | Why it flips the hash |
| --- | --- |
| `.cargo/config.toml` `rustflags` / `linker` changes (e.g. toggling `rust-lld`) | Linker + rustflags are part of every unit's metadata hash — a full-graph invalidation |
| `RUSTFLAGS` / `CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS` set for some invocations and not others | Same as above, but flips **back and forth** — the worst case, rebuilds on every alternation |
| `[profile.dev]` / `[profile.release]` edits (`debug`, `opt-level`, `codegen-units`, `lto`) | Profile settings feed the hash |
| `Cargo.toml` dependency / `[patch]` churn that changes feature unification reaching `aws-lc-fips-sys` | Enabled feature set is part of the hash |
| An **uncommitted** or per-developer `.cargo/config.local.toml` that appears/disappears | Silently flips rustflags between runs |
| `cargo clean`, deleting `target/`, "let's start fresh" | Destroys the artifacts outright |
| Changing `CC` / `CXX` / `CMAKE` / `GOPATH` / `AWS_LC_*` in the environment | The build script declares `rerun-if-env-changed` on these |

A healthy host accumulates **at most one** `aws-lc-fips-sys-<hash>/out`
directory per `(profile, target)` pair. If `ls target/*/build/` shows
many of them, the workflow has been thrashing and each extra directory
is several wasted minutes.

## Diagnostic

```powershell
# How many full aws-lc FIPS builds has this host paid for?
Get-ChildItem target\debug\build -Directory -Filter "aws-lc-fips-sys-*" |
  Where-Object { Test-Path (Join-Path $_.FullName "out\build\artifacts") } |
  Select-Object Name, LastWriteTime
```

Before and after any build-configuration change, run this. If the count
increased, the change was an invalidator — say so plainly in your
report rather than absorbing it silently.

## Forbidden actions

- **`cargo clean`** — in any repo under `c:\analog-pim\`, for any
  reason. There is no development problem whose correct solution is
  wiping `target/`. If you believe the cache is corrupt, delete the
  *specific* offending crate's fingerprint directory
  (`target/<profile>/.fingerprint/<crate>-*`) and never
  `aws-lc-fips-sys-*`.
- **Deleting `target/**/aws-lc-fips-sys-*`**, its `out/`, or its
  `build/artifacts/` tree — including "disk cleanup" sweeps.
- **Deleting staged FIPS DLLs** (`target/<profile>/aws_lc_fips_*_crypto.dll`
  and the `deps/` copy) — see
  [`fips-dll-staging.mdc`](fips-dll-staging.mdc). Removing them forces
  `build.rs` to re-run and re-locate artifacts unnecessarily.
- **Gratuitous `rustflags` / `linker` / `[profile.*]` churn.** These
  settings are committed precisely so they stay put. Do not "try"
  a linker or profile tweak on a whim; a single experiment costs a
  full-graph rebuild in **both** directions (once to apply, once to
  revert).
- **Setting `RUSTFLAGS` in a shell, script, or task** for a one-off
  build. Put the flag in the committed `.cargo/config.toml` or do not
  use it at all. Per
  [`no-environment-variables.mdc`](no-environment-variables.mdc), env
  vars are not a configuration surface in this workspace anyway.
- **Feature toggling that changes `aws-lc-fips-sys`'s own feature set.**
  Per `enterprise-compliance.mdc` §4 the FIPS posture is hard-coded, not
  a Cargo feature — so there is nothing legitimate to toggle here.
  (Crate-local features that do *not* reach `aws-lc-fips-sys`, such as
  `skip-ui-build`, are fine.)

## Allowed

- **Exactly one** build per `(profile, target)` pair, on first use of
  that combination on a host — e.g. one `dev` build and one `release`
  build. That is the "unavoidable, build it once" case.
- Keeping **both** the `dev` and `release` aws-lc trees warm
  simultaneously. Do not delete one to make room for the other.
- A deliberate, announced, one-time invalidation when a build-config
  change is genuinely required (e.g. adopting a linker that measurably
  helps). Announce the cost up front, make the change **once**, commit
  it, and never revert casually — reverting costs the same rebuild
  again.

## Reverting a past invalidator is usually the wrong fix

If a previous commit already paid for a rebuild and the new fingerprint
is now stable and cached, **leave it alone**. Reverting the setting
would force a *second* full rebuild to get back to the old hash. The
cost is sunk; the correct action is to stop churning, not to undo. Only
revert if the setting is actively flipping the hash on repeated runs.

